@node Dataflow
@chapter Dataflow

@cindex dataflow
@cindex patterns
A mode's @code{pattern} chooses how it is laid out on the device mesh (default 4x4).  The single
register per PE and edge-only operand injection (north per column, west per row)
are the constraints every pattern works within.

@table @code
@item diagonal
@cindex diagonal pattern
Element-wise binary ops.  @var{a} enters from the north edge, @var{b} from the
west; PEs above the diagonal @code{pass} @var{a} southward, those below
@code{pass} @var{b} eastward, and each diagonal PE computes one result.  @code{min(rows,cols)}
lanes per transaction; @code{out = diag}.

@item reduce
@cindex reduce pattern
Accumulation into PE(0,0) with @code{mac}/@code{acc}: one element pair per step,
folded into the register, giving a scalar (e.g.@: @code{dot}).

@item custom
@cindex custom pattern
You place each PE with @code{pe R,C = @dots{}}; unlisted PEs are @code{nop}.
Output taps default to the diagonal, or a single @code{pe R,C} via @code{out}.

@item systolic
@cindex systolic pattern
@cindex weight-stationary
Weight-stationary matrix--vector @code{y = A*x}, driven by @command{cgra
matvec}.  The vector @var{x} is held stationary in the PE immediates; the
matrix streams in one row per step as a wavefront marching south (row 0
multiplies, the remaining rows form a delay line).  The array is tiled over rows x cols blocks
for any @code{M x N}; the host sums the skewed partials per output.

@item conv
@cindex convolution
1-D convolution as a Toeplitz matrix--vector (@command{cgra conv}).

@item scan
@cindex prefix scan
Inclusive prefix scan on row 0 (@command{cgra scan}): the block's elements
arrive on the north edge and the running prefix flows west to
east, tiled over columns with the carry chained between blocks.
@end table

@cindex stationary data
@section Local accumulation and forwarding limits
Output-stationary means that a partial sum stays local while products are
accumulated.  The MAC instruction already supports this: @command{cgra dot}
keeps its running sum in PE(0,0).

Each PE exposes only one result register to its neighbours.  It cannot retain
a sum and simultaneously forward independent activation and weight values
from that register.  This limits a one-MAC-per-PE-per-step schedule;
other schedules can use routing PEs, multiple phases or extra storage.

The implemented matrix mapping keeps coefficients in row 0, where C PEs
multiply.  The remaining (R-1)*C PEs delay the products, and the host reduces
each output row.  In the default 4x4 array that is four active multipliers
and twelve delay cells.  Convolution skips zero tiles on the wire but still
materialises a dense Toeplitz matrix in host memory.
